## A model of taste

<div class="todo">

Working title. The most technical main section, where the post's ramp ends.

- **Planned** · logistic matrix factorisation (notebook stub, Johnson 2014). **Goes here**: P(cool) = σ(item coolness + device leniency + device taste · item taste), one line per term
- **Fix** · the stub writes `θu^τ βi`; it means the dot product of the device's and the item's latent vectors
- **Suggested** · start with no taste vectors at all: that is the Rasch model from psychometrics (item difficulty ↔ coolness, person ability ↔ leniency). It gives a leaderboard corrected for who happened to vote on what; show where it disagrees with the raw one
- **Suggested** · choose the number of dimensions by held-out error: hide 10–20 % of votes, compare log-loss / AUC at 0, 1, 2, 3 dimensions. If 1–2 beat 0, taste dimensions exist; this curve is stronger evidence for "opinion clusters" than a silhouette score
- **Suggested** · Johnson's LMF is built for implicit feedback (play counts); these are explicit ±1 votes with real gaps, so plain logistic MF with L2 (1-bit matrix completion) is the closer fit. Cite accordingly
- **Suggested** · payoff for readers: "the model predicts your next swipe X % of the time", and an item's nearest neighbours in taste space

</div>

