### <span id=t-test class = 'def'> Coolness </span>

An item's like count follows a binomial distribution. 
For each item, an exact binomial test checks whether its like rate differs from the baseline. Many items have only 15–20 votes, where normal approximations become unreliable. Testing about 270 items at once will increase false positives, since around $\lceil 271 \cdot 0.05\rceil \approx 14$ items would be significant by chance. The Benjamini–Hochberg (BH) procedure corrects for this by controlling the False Discovery Rate (FDR). It keeps the expected share of spurious findings among significant items at 5%.

```python
from scipy.stats import binomtest, false_discovery_control

baseline = 0.77
items = stats.drop(index="Sum")   # don't test the Sum row

p = items.apply(
    lambda r: binomtest(int(r.up), int(r.up + r.down), p=baseline).pvalue,
    axis=1,
)

stats = stats.assign(
    p_value=p,
    p_adj=pd.Series(false_discovery_control(p), index=p.index),  # Benjamini–Hochberg
```

